\version "2.24.0"
% COUNTERSTATEMENT, bars 27-42, ff. Theme F1 in the bass (cellos, violas, bassoons, horns 3-4; basses an octave lower);
% the violins play a running countersubject in eighths (Adler part 2 PDF 172-186: polyphonic texture, foreground and counterline).
% 35-38: the head in the violins in A flat, then B flat minor; 39-42: V of E flat, diminuendo into the transition.
% Woodwinds and horns 1-2 hold the harmony; trombones join at 35. No repeated-eighth hammering in the basses: that layout is
% movement I's first-theme tutti and is kept for the coda (cyclic-notes 'Owed' item 7).

% c032 (harmony; A&S Unit 17, PDF 267: voices above a sequential bass follow the sequence, or 5ths appear): 32 Fl 1, Ob 2, Cl 1 G-F-A flat (were G-A flat, parallel 5ths with the bass statement's C-D flat); 35 Fl 2 and Ob 1 C5 (were E flat5: D-E flat over G-A flat, parallel 5ths in the V-VI move into the A flat statement); 38 Fl 2 and Ob 1 C5-D5, Cl 1 E flat4-D4 takes the seventh (were D flat-E flat, 5ths with Vln II's G flat-A flat).
% c132 (harmony; A&S Unit 22, PDF 377 and 383 point 4: a seventh enters as passing tone, common tone or neighbour): 37 Cl 1 B flat4 dotted half-F4 on beat 4 (after the violins' B flat-F, so no octaves with them), so the 7th E flat4 of ii7 at 38 comes by step from F (was a leap of a 5th from B flat4).
% c208 (expression; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. IV, PDF 129-130 and Ex. 254, PDF 418: a note is stressed by a chain of three or four small unstressed notes in scale form, an upward glide slurred to it, in strings or wood-wind, not into string chords of three or four notes): 35 Vln I-II (unison, single notes) glide E flat-F-G into A flat 5, the head of F1 on VI, the counterstatement's first new key, so the violins' change from countersubject (27-34) to theme is heard; F1's notes and rhythm unchanged, Fl 1 keeps the plain note. Not at 37: a glide F-G-A flat into B flat 5 makes outer-voice octaves A flat-B flat with the bass (36.3-37).
% c208 (Rimsky-Korsakov PDF 136: after a long rest the trombones should re-enter pp or ff, 'piano and forte re-entries are less successful'; PDF 129: sf-dim): 35 Tbn 1-2, B.tbn (silent since bar 6) and the Cbsn they double attack ff and fall to f within bar 35 (the f on beat 4, so bar 36 carries no new mark); the timpani stroke stays f.
pTwoVlnOne = { c'''8(\ff bes'' aes'' g'') f''( d'' b' d'') | bes'8( d'' f'' aes'') c'''( aes'' f'' c'') | b'8( d'' f'' d'') g''( f'' es'' d'') | b'8( c'' d'' f'') es''( g'' c''' es''') | d'''8( c''' bes'' g'') es''( g'' bes'' es''') | des'''8( c''' bes'' aes'') f''( aes'' c''' f''') | aes''8( g'' f'' es'') d''( f'' aes'' c''') | b''8( c''' d''' b'' g''4) r4 | \grace { es''16( f'' g'' } aes''2-> es''4. f''8 | g''4-.) f''8( es''8 des''4-.) c''4-. | bes''2->( f''4. ges''8 | aes''4-.) ges''8( f''8 es''4-.) d''4-. | f''2->(\> bes'4. c''8 | d''4-.) c''8( bes'8 aes'4-.) g'4-. | f'2( d'4. es'8 | f'2.)\p r4 | }
% c097 (texture; Adler ch. 11, PDF 42-44, Hindemith Symphonic Metamorphoses ii 158-196: every instrument in its best register 'so that the subject comes through clearly every time'; the voices around the subject drop to p): 27-38 Vln II plays the countersubject at Vln I's pitch. Its lower octave (B flat3-E flat5) ran through the bass statement's register (C4-A flat4 in Hn 3-4, bassoons, violas, cellos), crossing under its F4 peak at 30 and meeting it at the unison at 28, 29, 30 and 33; at 35-38 it lay under the violas' and cellos' countersubject. The lower octave returns at 39. 27 the woodwind and Hn 1-2 pads mf (were f) under the ff subject and countersubject.
pTwoVlnTwo = { c'''8(\ff^\markup \italic "unis. with Vln I" bes'' aes'' g'') f''( d'' b' d'') | bes'8( d'' f'' aes'') c'''( aes'' f'' c'') | b'8( d'' f'' d'') g''( f'' es'' d'') | b'8( c'' d'' f'') es''( g'' c''' es''') | d'''8( c''' bes'' g'') es''( g'' bes'' es''') | des'''8( c''' bes'' aes'') f''( aes'' c''' f''') | aes''8( g'' f'' es'') d''( f'' aes'' c''') | b''8( c''' d''' b'' g''4) r4 | \grace { es''16( f'' g'' } aes''2-> es''4. f''8 | g''4-.) f''8( es''8 des''4-.) c''4-. | bes''2->( f''4. ges''8 | aes''4-.) ges''8( f''8 es''4-.) d''4-. | f'2->(\> bes4. c'8 | d'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f'2( d'4. es'8 | f'2.)\p r4 | }
pTwoVc = { \clef tenor c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | es'8( des'8 c'8 bes8) aes8( c'8 es'8 aes'8) | bes'8( g'8 es'8 des'8) c'8( es'8 aes'8 c'8) | f'8( es'8 des'8 c'8) bes8( des'8 f'8 bes8) | c'8( aes8 f8 aes8) f8( d'8 f'8 bes8) | \clef bass bes,1\> | bes,1 | bes,1 | bes,2.\p r4 | }
pTwoVla = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | es'8( des'8 c'8 bes8) aes8( c'8 es'8 aes'8) | bes'8( g'8 es'8 des'8) c'8( es'8 aes'8 c'8) | f'8( es'8 des'8 c'8) bes8( des'8 f'8 bes8) | c'8( aes8 f8 aes8) f8( d'8 f'8 d'8) | d'1\> | d'1 | aes1 | f2.\p r4 | }
pTwoBsnOne = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | aes1 | g2 aes2 | des'1 | c'2 d'2 | d'1~\> | d'1~ | d'1~ | d'2.\p r4 | }
pTwoBsnTwo = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | aes,1 | es2 aes,2 | bes,1 | f,2 bes,2 | bes,1~\> | bes,1~ | bes,1~ | bes,2.\p r4 | }
pTwoHnThree = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | c'1 | bes2 c'2 | des'1 | c'2 d'2 | f'1~\> | f'1~ | f'1~ | f'2.\p r4 | }
pTwoHnFour = { c'2->(\ff g4. aes8 | bes4-.) aes8( g8 f4-.) es4-. | d4( g8 aes8 b4 d'4) | f'2-> es'4( d'4) | es'2->( bes4. c'8 | des'4-.) c'8( bes8 aes4-.) g4-. | f4( aes8 c'8 f'4 aes'4) | g'2.-> r4 | aes1 | g2 aes2 | bes1 | aes2 bes2 | bes1~\> | bes1~ | bes1~ | bes2.\p r4 | }
% c166 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 26: the double bass's practical top is G (written); higher notes with caution and never by skip): 33 beat 4 basses repeat F3 (written F4) instead of leaping to A flat 3 (written A flat 4); the A flat stays in the cellos, violas, bassoons and Hn 3-4.
pTwoCb = { \clef tenor c2->(\ff g,4. aes,8 | bes,4-.) aes,8( g,8 f,4-.) es,4-. | d,4( g,8 aes,8 b,4 d4) | f2-> es4( d4) | es2->( bes,4. c8 | des4-.) c8( bes,8 aes,4-.) g,4-. | f,4( aes,8 c8 f4 f4) | g2.-> r4 | \clef bass aes,,1 | es,2 aes,,2 | bes,,1 | f,2 bes,,2 | bes,,1\> | bes,,1 | bes,,1 | bes,,2.\p r4 | }
% c075 (MOLA Example 3, PDF 10): the contrabassoon re-enters at 35 after two bars' rest at f, with the bass trombone it doubles
% c187 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 77: D. basses + Bass cl. among the best unison pairs; PDF 80, Ex. 96 and Servilia 168: the bass octave of a low melody
% doubled by a reed): 27-34 the bass clarinet doubles the basses' lower octave of the bass statement (C3), which was the basses alone under six players at C4.
% 33 it takes the cellos' A flat (the basses repeat F, c166). The bass clarinet is otherwise silent from 7 to 143.
pTwoBassCl = { c2->(\ff g,4. aes,8 | bes,4-.) aes,8( g,8 f,4-.) es,4-. | d,4( g,8 aes,8 b,4 d4) | f2-> es4( d4) | es2->( bes,4. c8 | des4-.) c8( bes,8 aes,4-.) g,4-. | f,4( aes,8 c8 f4 aes4) | g2.-> r4 | R1*8 | }
pTwoCbsn = { c,2->(\ff g,,4. aes,,8 | bes,,4-.) aes,,8( g,,8 f,,4-.) es,,4-. | d,,4( g,,8 aes,,8 b,,4 d,4) | f,2-> es,4( d,4) | es,2->( bes,,4. c,8 | des,4-.) c,8( bes,,8 aes,,4-.) g,,4-. | R1*2 | aes,,2.~\ff\> aes,,4\f | es,2 aes,,2 | bes,,1 | f,2 bes,,2 | bes,,1~\> | bes,,1 | R1*2\! | }
% c188 (Adler ch. 10, adler1 PDF 309 and 321: slurred notes are one breath, separate notes are tongued; Ex. X-15, X-16, PDF 306-307: a sustained soft horn/wind choir is held, not re-attacked):
% held pitches in the wind and brass pads are tied over the barline (28-34) and through the staged diminuendo 39-42 (re-attacked once, at 39, where it starts), so the B flat pedal fades without four fresh attacks; Hn 1-2 breathe after 38. Notes and dynamics unchanged.
% c201 (melody; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 76-77: Vln + Fl is a natural unison and the strings predominate; PDF 67: doublings belong to loud passages; PDF 54: a melody stands out as the top part): 35-38 F1's head (Vln I+II unison, ff) lay under Fl 1's held chord tones and was not the top line. Fl 1 now doubles the violins' head at the unison, f, with their articulation; Fl 2 keeps its held notes, marked f with it (shared staff). The octave-above layout of PDF 78 cost a page.
% c209 (Rimsky-Korsakov ch. II, PDF 76-77: a wood-wind in unison with a string line amplifies it only when it plays the same line; Ex. 3, PDF 177, and Ex. 12, PDF 186: the flute doubling a violin melody follows its ornaments): 35 Fl 1 (c201's unison doubling of F1's head with Vln I-II) takes the violins' grace glide E flat-F-G (c208), so the doubled attack on A flat5 is one sound, not a plain flute note against the violins' glide. Fl 2 gets matching grace skips (shared staff). Notes otherwise unchanged.
pTwoFluteOne = { g''1\mf | bes''2 aes''2 | g''1~ | g''1~ | g''1 | f''2 aes''2~ | aes''1 | g''2. r4 | \grace { es''16( f'' g'' } aes''2->\f es''4. f''8 | g''4-.) f''8( es''8 des''4-.) c''4-. | bes''2->( f''4. ges''8 | aes''4-.) ges''8( f''8 es''4-.) d''4-. | bes''1 | aes''1~\> | aes''1~ | aes''1\p | }
pTwoFluteTwo = { es''2\mf d''2~ | d''2 f''2 | d''1~ | d''2 es''2~ | es''1 | f''1~ | f''2 d''2~ | d''2. r4 | \grace { s16 s16 s16 } c''1\f | es''1 | des''1 | c''2 bes'2 | d''1~ | d''1~\> | d''1~ | d''1\p | }
pTwoOboeOne = { es''2\mf d''2~ | d''2 f''2 | d''1~ | d''2 es''2~ | es''1 | f''1~ | f''2 d''2~ | d''2. r4 | c''1 | des''2 es''2 | des''1 | c''2 bes'2 | d''1~ | d''1~\> | d''1~ | d''1\p | }
pTwoOboeTwo = { g'1\mf | bes'2 aes'2 | g'1~ | g'1~ | g'1 | f'2 aes'2~ | aes'1 | g'2. r4 | aes'1 | g'2 aes'2 | bes'1 | aes'2 bes'2~ | bes'1 | aes'1~\> | aes'1~ | aes'1\p | }
pTwoClOne = { g'1\mf | f'2 aes'2 | f'1~ | f'2 g'2~ | g'1 | f'2 aes'2~ | aes'2 f'2 | g'2. r4 | aes'1 | g'2 aes'2 | bes'2. f'4 | es'2 d'2 | f'1~ | f'1~\> | f'1~ | f'1\p | }
pTwoClTwo = { c'2\mf b2 | bes2 c'2 | b1~ | b2 c'2~ | c'2 bes2 | des'2 c'2~ | c'1 | b2. r4 | c'1 | bes2 c'2 | bes1 | c'2 bes2 | bes1~ | bes1~\> | bes1~ | bes1\p | }
% c166 (playability; Rimsky-Korsakov ch. I, PDF 37 and 44: wind and brass players must be given a little rest from time to time): 34 Hn 1-2 rest on beat 4 with every other part (they played 11-42 with only the rests at 18.4 and 26.3-4, 27-42 being 23 s of held notes).
pTwoHnOne = { g'1\mf | f'1~ | f'1~ | f'2 g'2~ | g'1 | f'1~ | f'1 | g'2. r4 | aes'1 | g'2 aes'2 | f'1~ | f'1 \breathe | f'1~\> | f'1~ | f'1~\p | f'1 | }
pTwoHnTwo = { c'2\mf b2 | bes2 c'2 | b1~ | b2 c'2~ | c'2 bes2 | des'2 c'2~ | c'1 | b2. r4 | c'1 | bes2 c'2 | bes1 | aes2 bes2 \breathe | bes1~\> | bes1~ | bes1~\p | bes1 | }
pTwoTbnOne = { R1*8 | es2.~\ff\> es4~\f | es2 es2 | des1 | c2 d2 | f1~\> | f1 | R1*2\! | }
pTwoTbnTwo = { R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | R1 | c2.~\ff\> c4\f | bes,2 c2 | bes,1 | aes,2 bes,2 | d1~\> | d1 | R1\! | R1 | }
% c161 (Adler ch. 11, PDF 28 item 3 and Ex. XI-12, Schubert Rosamunde Entr'acte I 106-23: the trombones' diminuendo is scored by dropping two trombones and leaving the third
% to bolster the low strings): 41-42 the bass trombone holds B flat2 with the cellos and Bn 2 into the p of 42 (it stopped with Tbn 1-2 after 40), so the B flat pedal under F1's last head keeps one brass voice to the end of the counterstatement.
pTwoBassTbn = { R1*8 | aes,2.~\ff\> aes,4\f | es2 aes,2 | bes,1 | f,2 bes,2 | bes,1~\> | bes,1~ | bes,1~ | bes,2.\p r4 | }
pTwoTimp = { c4->\ff r4 r2 | R1*3 | c4-> r4 r2 | R1 | R1 | g,4-> g,4 g,4 r4 | aes,4->\f r4 r2 | es4 r4 aes,4 r4 | R1*6 | }
